# WebSocket server created on default port even when HMR is disabled

## Environment

- Vite: 7.3.0
- Node: 22.x
- OS: macOS

## Description

When running Vite in middleware mode (as Nuxt does), the SSR environment has `hmr: false`, but `createWebSocketServer` still attempts to create a standalone WebSocket server on port 24678.

This causes port conflicts when running multiple Vite-based apps simultaneously.

## Reproduction

1. Run Vite in middleware mode with multiple environments
2. One environment (client) has HMR enabled, another (SSR) has `hmr: false`
3. SSR environment still tries to create WebSocket on port 24678
4. Error: `WebSocket server error: Port 24678 is already in use`

## Root Cause

In `createWebSocketServer` (ws.ts), the condition for creating a standalone WebSocket server is:

```js
if (wsServer) {
  // Attach to existing server
} else {
  // Create standalone WebSocket - ALWAYS runs when wsServer is falsy
  const route = ...
  wsHttpServer.listen(port, host);
}
```

When `hmr: false` (SSR environment), `wsServer` is `false` (since `hmrServer` is not set), so it falls into the `else` branch and tries to create a standalone WebSocket on port 24678.

## Expected Behavior

When `hmr: false`, no WebSocket server should be created at all.

## Proposed Fix

```js
if (wsServer) {
  // Attach to existing server
} else if (hmr !== false) {
  // Only create standalone WebSocket when HMR is enabled
  const route = ...
  wsHttpServer.listen(port, host);
}
```

## Related

- Nuxt issue: https://github.com/nuxt/cli/issues/1170 (HMR port conflict in monorepo)

## Workaround

Patch `vite/dist/node/chunks/config.js` to add `if (hmr !== false)` check before creating standalone WebSocket server.

### Description

We are using rolldown with vite 8.

The main problem right now is that the default strategy is product thousands of tiny chunks.

Example: If you open https://contra.com/gajus, currently, its loading 575 chunks. That's a lot.

And most of them are _tiny_

<img width="867" height="965" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7393d61f-d5c8-4bf4-b5e7-b3e8af39da69" />

What we need is some sane strategy for chunk merging.

### Suggested solution

I've spent a considerable amount of time trying to figure out a chunking strategy that would just work, but to no avail, but I am running into several issues:

1. Using `advancedChunks` produces broken bundles due to side-effects.
    Example of an error:
    ```
