### Explain in Detail

There are two types of TypeScript files: Source files (`.ts`, `.tsx`, `.cts`, `.mts`) and declaration files (`.d.ts`, `.d.cts`, `.d.mts`, `.d.*.ts`).

Declaration files describe a JavaScript file. Typically users are supposed to author source files, and if relevant, emit declaration files as a build artifact. Users are typically **not** supposed to author declaration files. Yet, [Vite recommends authoring a file named `vite-env.d.ts`](https://vite.dev/guide/env-and-mode#intellisense-for-typescript).

The practical problem often comes from users who don’t understand these differences and TypeScript compiler options. It’s common to enable the option [`skipLibCheck`](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#skipLibCheck). This option disables type checking of declaration files. So users end up with a file named `vite-env.d.ts` that’s not type-checked. Another confusing aspect is TypeScript scripts vs modules. The option [`moduleDetection`](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#moduleDetection) affects source files, but not declaration files.

The convention to create a file named `{tool}-env.d.ts` is not limited to Vite. I believe there are also `astro-env.d.ts` and `next-env.d.ts`. These frameworks suffer from the same problems.

### Your Suggestion for Changes

I suggest to remove all references to `vite-env.d.ts`, and replace them with `vite-env.ts`. This file should also be explicitly marked as a module to avoid ambiguity. This includes the documentation as well as all project templates.

```ts
// vite-env.ts
/// <reference types="vite/client" />

declare global {
  interface ImportMetaEnv {
    readonly VITE_APP_TITLE: string
    // more env variables...
  }

  interface ImportMeta {
    readonly env: ImportMetaEnv
 }
}

// Explicitly mark this file as a module.
export {}
```

